{"texts": ["1  Industry analyst preparing report: Heidi Colby-Oizumi (202-205-3391); Tariff Affairs contact: Dan Shepherdson (202-205-\n2598).\n2  Access to an electronic copy of this memorandum is available at http://www.usitc.gov/tata/hts/other/rel_doc/bill_reports/.\n3  The sponsor/proponent did not identify any additional beneficiaries of this bill.\nUNITED STATES INTERNATIONAL TRADE COMMISSION\nWashington, DC  20436\nMEMORANDUM ON PROPOSED TARIFF LEGISLATION\nof the 110th Congress 1\n[Date approved:  March 27, 2008]2\nBill No. and sponsor:  H.R. 4476 (Mr. Joe Courtney of Connecticut).\nProponent name,3 location:  Warren Corporation, Stafford Springs, CT.\nOther bills on product (110th Congress only):  None.\nNature of bill:  Extension of temporary duty suspension through December 31, 2011.\nRetroactive effect:  None.\nSuggested article description(s) for enactment (including appropriate HTS subheading(s)):\nYarn of combed cashmere or yarn of camel hair (provided for in subheading 5108.20.80).\nCheck one: X    Same as that in bill as introduced.\n       Different from that in bill as introduced (see Technical comments section).\nProduct information, including uses/applications and source(s) of imports:\nThe bill covers yarns made wholly of combed cashmere and/or camel hair and yarns made in blends of\ncombed cashmere or camel hair in which the cashmere or camel hair predominates by weight over any\nother single textile fiber.  These yarns are used to make worsted fabrics for men\u2019s and women\u2019s jackets\nand coats.  Worsted yarns are smooth-surfaced yarns spun from evenly combed long-staple fibers or, in\nthis case, fine animal hair.  The yarns and resulting woven fabrics have a smooth, firm surface, with little\nor no nap.  The subject yarns are produced from fibers that have undergone carding and combing before\nbeing spun into worsted or combed yarns.  Carding disentangles the fibers to prepare them for spinning\nand is done by passing the fibers between rollers covered with fine teeth.  Combing removes the shorter\nfibers and further aligns the longer ones to produce tops, which are then spun into combed yarns.\nThe subject yarns are provided for in HTS subheading 5108.20.80, which covers yarns of combed fine\nanimal hair, other than Angora rabbit hair and mohair.  In 2007, U.S. imports under HTS subheading\n5108.20.80 totaled $3.8 million (68,500 kilograms).  Italy was the primary supplier of such yarns,\nfollowed by Peru and France.\n2\nEstimated effect on customs revenue:\nHTS subheading:  5108.20.80\n2009 2010 2011 2012 2013\nCol. 1-General rate\nof duty (AVE) 4% 4% 4% 4% 4%\nEstimated value \ndutiable imports $3,000,000 $3,000,000 $3,000,000 $3,000,000 $3,000,000\nCustoms revenue\nloss 1/ $0 $120,000 $120,000 $120,000 $120,000\n1/  There is an existing duty suspension under HTS heading 9902.03.01 that expires on December 31,\n2009.  Therefore, there will be no customs revenue loss related to this bill in 2009.\nSource of estimated dutiable import data:  Commission estimates.\nContacts with domestic firms/organizations (including the proponent):\nName of firm/organization Date\ncontacted\nClaim US\nmakes same or\ncompeting\nproduct(s)?\nSubmission\nattached? \nOpposition\nnoted?\n(Yes/No)\nWarren Corporation (Proponent)\nLisa Cornish, 860-684-2766 (ext.  113)\n2/22/2008 No Yes No\nAmerican Manufacturing Trade Action\nCoalition\nSara Ormand, 202-452-0866\n2/28/2008 No Yes No\nHanora Spinning\nGerald Beyer, Fax: 401-765-6991\n2/26/2008 No No No\nJagger Brothers\nDavid Jagger, 207-324-5622\n2/26/2008 No No No\nKent Manufacturing\nMark Kent, 864-878-6367 (ext.  2525)\n2/26/2008 No No No\nNational Council of Textile Organizations\nDavid Trumbull, 202-822-8026\n2/26/2008 No No No\nNational Textile Association\nDavid Trumbull, 617-542-8220\n2/26/2008 No Yes No\nTailored Clothing Association\nDavid Starr, dastarr@wms-jen.com\n2/26/2008 No No No\nVictor Woollen Products, Ltd.\nTerry Winkler\n2/26/2008 No No No", "David Trumbull, 617-542-8220\n2/26/2008 No Yes No\nTailored Clothing Association\nDavid Starr, dastarr@wms-jen.com\n2/26/2008 No No No\nVictor Woollen Products, Ltd.\nTerry Winkler\n2/26/2008 No No No\n4  The Commission may express an opinion on the HTS classification of a product to facilitate consideration of the bill. However,\nby law, only the U.S. Customs Service is authorized to issue a binding ruling on this matter.  The Commission believes that the\nU.S. Customs Service should be consulted prior to enactment of the bill.\n3\nTechnical comments:4\nNone.\nI \n110TH CONGRESS \n1ST SESSION H. R. 4476 \nTo extend the temporary suspension of duty on yarn of combed cashmere \nor yarn of camel hair. \nIN THE HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES \nDECEMBER 12, 2007 \nMr. COURTNEY introduced the following bill; which was referred to the \nCommittee on Ways and Means \nA BILL \nTo extend the temporary suspension of duty on yarn of \ncombed cashmere or yarn of camel hair. \nBe it enacted by the Senate and House of Representa-1\ntives of the United States of America in Congress assembled, 2\nSECTION 1. YARN OF COMBED CASHMERE OR YARN OF 3\nCAMEL HAIR. 4\n(a) IN GENERAL.\u2014Heading 9902.03.01 of the Har-5\nmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States is amended 6\nby striking \u2018\u201812/31/2009\u2019\u2019 and inserting \u2018\u201812/31/2011\u2019\u2019. 7\n(b) E FFECTIVE DATE.\u2014The amendment made by 8\nsubsection (a) applies with respect to goods entered, or 9\nVerDate Aug 31 2005 21:32 Dec 13, 2007 Jkt 069200 PO 00000 Frm 00001 Fmt 6652 Sfmt 6201 E:\\BILLS\\H4476.IH H4476mstockstill on PROD1PC66 with BILLS\n2 \n\u2022HR 4476 IH\nwithdrawn from warehouse for consumption, on or after 1\nthe 15th day after the date of the enactment of this Act. 2\n\u00c6 \nVerDate Aug 31 2005 21:32 Dec 13, 2007 Jkt 069200 PO 00000 Frm 00002 Fmt 6652 Sfmt 6301 E:\\BILLS\\H4476.IH H4476mstockstill on PROD1PC66 with BILLS"], "vectors": [[-0.0670182853937149, 0.04471387714147568, 0.054255068302154